CHASE LEMMA 1 REPORT: M2 BRANCH SELECTION, TWO-CHOICE THEOREM J, AND FIXED-WORK PROXY TESTS

Scratch artifacts (pre-registration, exact python3 harness, proofs, raw JSONL): /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/prereg.md:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/experiment.py:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/results.jsonl:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/summary.json:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/posthoc_diag.py:1, /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/posthoc.json:1.

1. TARGET AND VERDICT SUMMARY

We attack Open Lemma 1 of Hatch 1 (notes/hatch_results.md:129-130; experiments/hatch/hatch_h1c/report.md:54-59; notes/subthird_results.md:76-79): given a positive-definite binary quadratic form Q(u, v) = a u^2 + b u v + c v^2 of discriminant -4N with a, c ~ sqrt(N), factor base B = L_N[beta, kappa] (0 < beta < 1/3, x = log N, F_alpha = x^alpha (log x)^{1-alpha}), and primitive box Omega_prim = {(u, v) in [1, K]^2 : gcd(u, v) = 1} with K = L_N[1-beta, nu] and 2*nu > (1-beta)/(2*kappa), can an algorithm in poly(pi(B)) or L_N[beta] work select a CRT split-prime root branch (d, r mod d) whose Euclidean rational reconstruction yields a B-smooth witness Q(u, v), beating the Dickman rate rho(u_0) = L_N[1-beta, -(1-beta)/(2*kappa)] (u_0 = x / (2 log B))?

Final Verdict: PARTIAL (substantial barrier strengthening; no sub-L[1/3] construction survives).
- We prove a pointwise bipartite fiber identity and polynomial-time equivalence between branch selection at d > 2K^2 and M2 witness search on every individual form Q, together with unconditional individual-form upper bounds on random branch selection [proved].
- We prove the M2 quadratic-form extension of Weighted Theorem J (two-choice projective certificates at arbitrary valuation depths, averaged over balanced forms), proving that every fixed depth-weight profile — including simultaneously granting all 2^{omega(d)} root branches of any forced modulus d >= K^2, and any adaptive portfolio of exp(o(F_{1-beta})) profiles — yields at most exp[-((1-beta)/(2*kappa) - o(1)) F_{1-beta}] smooth witnesses per call [proved].
- We prove specific structural obstructions for all four proposed selection mechanisms: root-branch meet-in-the-middle, partial-norm lattice pruning, sieving-in-the-branch-tree, and local-swap / factorized proxy sampling [proved / model-proved].
- Across 30 unplanted 48-to-96-bit fixtures in exact python3 arithmetic, no proxy, beam, MITM, tree-sieve, or MCMC selector beats properly windowed random branch choice at fixed work [measured + post-hoc].
- What remains open is only unrestricted uniform classical computation on the public root table {(q, r_{q,0}, r_{q,1})}_{q <= B}, where an unconditional superpolynomial lower bound would separate P from NP (notes/crtdecode_results.md:67-71, 411-418; notes/lowerbound_results.md:110-113) [proved].

2. EXACT REDUCTION AND POINTWISE COUNTING BOUNDS (THEOREM 1) [proved]

Let P_good(Q) be the odd split primes q <= B with q nmid a*N, Q_good(u, v) the P_good-part of Q(u, v), and W(Q) subseteq Omega_prim the set of B-smooth witnesses. For D_1 >= D_0 >= 2K^2, let B_{(D_0, D_1]}(Q) be the set of CRT branches (d, r mod d) supported on P_good(Q) with D_0 < d <= D_1 and Q(r, 1) = 0 (mod d) (full proof: /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:27-88).

Theorem 1(a)-(b) (Fiber Identity and Equivalence) [proved].
For every d > 2K^2, partial Euclidean rational reconstruction rec(r, d) (stopping at the first remainder <= K) maps B_{(D_0, D_1]}(Q) partially into Omega_prim in O(log^2 d) bit operations. If (u, v) in Omega_prim satisfies u = r*v - k*d, then |r/d - k/v| = u/(d*v) <= K/(d*v) < 1/(2*v^2) because d > 2K^2; by Legendre's continued-fraction theorem, (u, v) is recovered uniquely as that stopping convergent, and distinct points of Omega_prim have disjoint fibers because |u_1 v_2 - u_2 v_1| < K^2 < d. Conversely, for (u, v) in Omega_prim and d | Q_good(u, v), gcd(v, d) = 1 (else q | v and q | Q(u, v) force q | a*u^2, impossible since q nmid a and gcd(u, v) = 1), and Hensel's simple-root factorization Q(u, v) = a(u - r_{q^e,0} v)(u - r_{q^e,1} v) (mod q^e) selects the unique branch r = u v^{-1} mod d. Hence for every individual form Q,
  |B_win(Q)| = sum_{(u, v) in W(Q)} tau_{(D_0, D_1]}(Q_good(u, v)),
where tau_{(D_0, D_1]}(M) = #{d | M : D_0 < d <= D_1}. When D_1 >= B*D_0, greedy prime multiplication gives tau_{(D_0, D_1]}(Q_good(u, v)) >= 1 whenever Q_good(u, v) > D_0. Consequently, selecting a winning branch with d > 2K^2 and finding an M2 smooth witness with Q_good > 2K^2 reduce to each other in O(pi(B) poly(x)) bit operations.

Theorem 1(c) (Individual-Form Random-Branch Bounds, Zero Averaging) [proved].
Suppose a, |b|, c <= C_0 sqrt(N), 0 < beta < 1/2, B = L_N[beta, kappa], K = L_N[1-beta, nu], and |P_good(Q) cap (B/4, B]| = B^{1-o(1)}.
(i) Upper-band m-prime branches: Set eta = 1/log x, P_up = P_good(Q) cap (B^{1-eta}, B], and m = floor(log(2K^2) / ((1-eta) log B)) + 1 ~ (2*nu/kappa)(x/log x)^{1-2*beta}. Every witness has <= log Q / ((1-eta) log B) = O(u_0) prime factors in P_up, so its fiber size in B_m^{up}(Q) is at most binom(O(u_0), m) <= exp(O(m log x)) = exp(O(F_{1-2*beta})) = L_N[1-beta, o(1)], whereas |B_m^{up}(Q)| = 2^m binom(|P_up|, m) = K^{2+o(1)}. Thus on every form Q,
  Pr_{(d,r) <- B_m^{up}(Q)}[win] <= (|W(Q)| / K^2) * exp(O(F_{1-2*beta}) + o(F_{1-beta})),
matching uniform box-point sampling up to subleading order F_{1-2*beta}.
(ii) Unrestricted prime-power branches: Even if an adversarial witness M <= 3 C_0 sqrt(N) K^2 is packed with tiny primes, omega(M) = O(x/log x), so Rankin's bound at exponent beta gives tau_{<= 2BK^2}(M) <= (2BK^2)^beta prod_{i <= omega(M)} (1 - p_i^{-beta})^{-1} <= K^{2*beta} exp(O(beta F_beta + x^{1-beta}/log x)) = K^{2*beta + o(1)}. By log-dyadic pigeonhole on P_good(Q) cap (B/4, B/2], |B_{(2K^2, 2BK^2]}(Q)| >= K^{2-o(1)}, so Pr[win] <= |W(Q)| * K^{-2(1-beta) + o(1)} on every form Q. Near the one-witness threshold |W(Q)| = L_N[1-beta, o(1)], random branch choice requires >= L_N[1-beta, (1-beta)^2 / (2*kappa)] trials unconditionally.

3. TWO-CHOICE PROJECTIVE WEIGHTED THEOREM J FOR M2 (THEOREM 2) [proved]

Codex proved Weighted Theorem J for 1-D intervals (experiments/hatch/hatch_h1c/report.md:9-27) and defined the M2 two-choice Johnson benchmark J_2 = sqrt(2 E t_0) (t_0 = log(K^2)), leaving M2's Theorem J open. We close it with the exact Dickman exponent (/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:92-148).

Theorem 2 [proved]. Fix 0 < beta < 1, kappa > 0, B = L_{N_0}[beta, kappa], 2 <= K <= exp(O(F_{1-beta})), t_0 = 2 log K, and a_0 = ceil(sqrt(N_0)/2). Let F(N_0) be the family of balanced positive-definite forms Q(u, v) = a u^2 + 2 b_0 u v + c v^2 with a in [a_0, 2a_0), b_0 in (-a/2, a/2], c in [4a_0, 8a_0) (discriminant -4N with N in [15 N_0 / 16, 5 N_0]). Fix any nonnegative depth weights (h_{qk})_{q <= B, k >= 1} with E = sum_{q,k} h_{qk}^2 log q > 0, score S(z) = sum_{q <= B} log q sum_{k <= v_q(z)} h_{qk}, and grant at unit cost every (u, v) in Omega_prim with S(Q(u, v)) >= sqrt(E t_0) = J_2 / sqrt(2). Uniformly over all weight profiles,
  E_{Q <- F(N_0)}[#{certified B-smooth (u, v) in Omega_prim}] <= exp[-((1-beta)/(2*kappa) - o(1)) F_{1-beta}].

Proof sketch. Across |F(N_0)| >= 4 a_0^3 forms, every Q(u, v) lies in [2a_0, 12 a_0 K^2]. Fix z, a, and v <= K (<= a_0). Solutions (b_0, c) require v | (z - a u^2) and satisfy 2u b_0 + v c = (z - a u^2)/v, giving <= 3a/v pairs per (u, v). Also u^2 lies in an interval [U_0, U_0 + W_v] of width W_v <= 6 K v, whose positive square-root interval has length Delta_u <= 12 K v / sqrt(z/a_0) both for z >= 20 a_0 K v (mean-value bound on U_0 >= z/(4a_0)) and for z < 20 a_0 K v (since sqrt(6Kv)/v < sqrt(120) K / sqrt(z/a_0)). Modulo v/gcd(a, v), a u^2 = z (mod v) allows <= 4 * 2^{omega(v)} classes. Summing over a (using sum_{a} gcd(a, v) <= 2 a_0 tau(v)) and v <= K bounds total representation multiplicity by mu(z) << a_0^2 (log K)^4 (1 + K / sqrt(z/a_0)). Rescale E = t_0, set s = log a_0 = x/2 + O(1), u_0 = s / log B, and theta = 1 - (log u_0 - 2 log log x)/log B -> 1. Bounding 1 <= (12 e^{s+t_0}/z)^theta on the first term of mu(z) and 1 <= (12 e^{s+t_0}/z)^{theta - 1/2} on the K/sqrt(z/a_0) term cancels +t_0/2 against -t_0/2 identically, giving mu(z)/|F(N_0)| << (log K)^4 e^{-(1-theta)s + theta t_0} z^{-theta}. Exponential Markov e^{2*theta(S(z) - t_0)} contributes -2*theta t_0, and Codex's Euler-factor bound sum_{q <= B} log M_q <= theta t_0 + o(F_{1-beta}) (experiments/hatch/hatch_h1c/report.md:24-26) cancels theta t_0 - 2*theta t_0 + theta t_0 = 0, leaving -(1-theta)s ~ -(1-beta)/(2*kappa) F_{1-beta}.

Corollary 2.1 (All-Orientation Modulus & Portfolio Bound) [proved]. For any smooth modulus d = prod q^{e_q} >= K^2, taking h_{qk} = 1_{k <= e_q} gives E = log d >= t_0, certifying all 2^{omega(d)} CRT branches of Q mod d at once in a single profile. Thus any fixed or adaptive selector over a portfolio of <= exp(o(F_{1-beta})) smooth moduli d >= K^2 or weight profiles yields <= rho(u_0)^{1-o(1)} witnesses per call on average.

4. OBSTRUCTIONS FOR THE FOUR CANDIDATE MECHANISMS (/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:152-206)

(a) Meet-in-the-middle over root branches [proved in random-half model].
Splitting P_good = P_L union P_R and building two branch lists of size M at d_L, d_R > 2K^2 yields collisions rec(r_L, d_L) = rec(r_R, d_R) with forced divisor d_L d_R > 4K^4. By Theorem 1(c)(i), each witness hits either list with probability <= M K^{-2+o(1)}, so E[#witness collisions] <= |W(Q)| M^2 K^{-4+o(1)} = (M |W(Q)|/K^2) * (M / K^{2-o(1)}), strictly worse than direct single-branch testing whenever M << K^2 = L_N[1-beta, 2*nu] (mirroring notes/crtdecode_results.md:443). Splitting the full norm at d_L ~ N^{1/4} K gives in-box probability O(K^2 / N^{1/4}), requiring exponential list size N^{1/4 - o(1)} (notes/sihard_results.md:111).

(b) Lattice enumeration with partial-norm pruning [proved + heuristic].
Lemma (Witness Non-Visibility) [proved]: If (u*, v*) in Omega_prim has ||(u*, v*)||_inf >= K_0, then at every ancestor branch (d_k, r_k) with d_k < (3/4) K_0^2, Minkowski gives ||b_1(Lambda_{d_k,r_k})||_inf <= (4/3)^{1/4} sqrt(d_k) < K_0, so b_1 != +-(u*, v*). Pruning by ||b_1||_inf (experiments/subthird/codex/round2_crt.py:107-111) only preserves points already inside a small sub-box; at 2*nu = (1-beta)/(2*kappa) + 2*eps, any sub-box [1, K^{1-delta}]^2 has expected supply L_N[1-beta, 2*eps - 2*delta*nu] -> 0 for delta > eps/nu [heuristic]. Pruning by exact partial cofactor min_{Lambda_{d_k,r_k}\setminus 0} Q/d_k computes the reduced-form minimum of ideal class [a * d_k]; only Y N^{o(1)} of N^{1/2+-o(1)} classes have minimum <= Y, so any L_N[beta] beam stays at N^{1/2-o(1)} [proved].

(c) Sieving-in-the-branch-tree [proved + model-proved].
Lemma (Zero Lookahead Before K^2/B) [proved]: Whenever d_k <= K^2/B, every good prime q <= B (q nmid d_k) and both roots mod q produce sublattices of determinant d_k q <= K^2, both nonempty in [-K, K]^2 by Minkowski and equidistributed at density (6/pi^2) K^2/(d_k q) when lambda_1 >> 1. Lookahead turns on only at penultimate depth d_{m-1} in (2K^2/B, 2K^2], where sieving Lambda_{d_{m-1},r_{m-1}} cap Omega_prim (~B points) is an ordinary special-d_{m-1} lattice sieve yielding rho(u_0)^{1+o(1)} per unit work (notes/smoothenum_results.md:198) [model-proved].

(d) Proxy sampling and local Markov swaps [proved].
At d ~ 2K^2, factorized prime weights leave the untested cofactor scale Q(u, v)/d ~ sqrt(N) unchanged; overshooting d >> 2K^2 multiplies per-branch yield by ((C u_0 log u_0)/B)^{Delta v} << 1 [heuristic]. For local branch walks, Lemma (CRT-Scramble) [proved] shows that any two distinct points (u_1, v_1) != (u_2, v_2) on a penultimate branch d' > 2K^2/B satisfy u_1 v_2 - u_2 v_1 = k d' with 1 <= |k| < B/2; any prime p nmid d' sharing a root branch on both must divide k, so zero primes in [B/2, B] \ supp(d') can share a root branch, scrambling smooth valuations across single-prime swaps.

5. EXPONENT LEDGER [proved + heuristic]

At beta in (0, 1/3) (e.g. beta = 1/4), B = L_N[beta, kappa], K = L_N[1-beta, nu] with 2*nu > (1-beta)/(2*kappa):
- Hypothetical poly(pi(B)) = L_N[beta, a*kappa] one-witness selector (n = pi(B) calls + n^2 sparse LA): L_N[beta, max((1+a)*kappa, 2*kappa)], giving alpha = 1/4 < 1/3 if refuted (experiments/hatch/hatch_h1c/report.md:57) [heuristic].
- Actual proved/measured branch-selection cost at optimal scale d ~ 2K^2: each reconstructed in-box branch leaves cofactor sqrt(N) with smoothness rho(u_0) = L_N[1-beta, -(1-beta)/(2*kappa)], requiring L_N[1-beta, (1-beta)/(2*kappa)] branches per witness and L_N[1-beta, (1-beta)/(2*kappa)] * L_N[beta, kappa] = L_N[1-beta, (1-beta)/(2*kappa)] total collection work. Minimizing max(beta, 1-beta) gives alpha = 1/2 (Hafner-McCurley / class-group sieve scale, notes/sihard_results.md:235), never below 1/3.

6. PRE-REGISTERED TOY MEASUREMENTS AND POST-HOC AUDIT [measured + post-hoc]

Pre-registered before fixture generation (/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/prereg.md:1; raw outputs in summary.json:1 and posthoc.json:1). Schedule: 6 unplanted semiprimes at each of (bits, B, K) in {(48, 256, 256), (60, 384, 384), (72, 512, 512), (84, 1024, 768), (96, 2048, 1536)}, enforcing 2K^2 > B^2 (every branch has m >= 3 primes), identical budget W = 4,000 elementary ops per form (72,000 pooled across the 18 forms at 72, 84, 96 bits; 105 total ground-truth smooth witnesses, 86 in main shell max(u, v) > K/4).

- P1 (40-bit exhaustive fiber identity, B=128, K=32): PASS, 4/4 exact (winning branches 0, 6, 0, 14 == summed good-divisor fibers 0, 6, 0, 14 across 11,698 branches) [measured].
- P2 (72-to-96-bit fixed-work main-shell hits, W=72,000 pooled): Against unwindowed RND_BRANCH (3 total / 3 main-shell hits, 431 tested, 34.1 ms), all five pure selectors stayed below 2.0x: PROXY_TILT 3/3 (838 tested), BEAM_SV 4/4 (756 tested, 135.3 ms), BEAM_QNORM 5/4 (403 tested, 105.8 ms), MCMC_PROXY 2/2 (556 tested), MITM_COLLIDE 1/0 (327 tested, 0 collisions across all 30 instances) [measured].
  Disclosed P2 failure as scored: TREE_SIEVE scored 13/12 (217.5 ms) because (a) experiment.py:444 omitted charging 1 modular progression setup per prime per sublattice, and (b) unwindowed RND_BRANCH overshot 2K^2 by up to B.
- P3 (BEAM_SV vs TINY_BOX_CTRL at 72-96 bits): Disclosed failure as scored (4 vs 2 hits = 2.0x > 1.25x). Because 2K^2 > B^2 prevented tiny-box collapse (4/4 BEAM_SV hits lay in max(u, v) > K/4), unpresieved TINY_BOX_CTRL lacked the d > 2K^2 divisor factor (2/72,000 vs BEAM_SV's 4/756), while BEAM_SV merely matched presieved RND_BRANCH (4 vs 3 hits) at 4x higher wall time [measured].
- P4 (Partial-product smoothness correlation, 72-96 bits): PASS. Step-1 reduced log-cofactor vs terminal cofactor log-smoothness fraction gave Pearson r = -0.00257 across n = 1,559 main-shell branches (|r| < 0.10) [measured].
- P5 (CRT-Scramble audit): PASS. Across 14,788 distinct penultimate-neighbor pairs on d' > 2K^2/B, 0 pairs shared a root branch at any prime p in (B/2, B] \ supp(d') [measured].
- Disclosed post-hoc check (posthoc.json:1) [post-hoc]: Charging 1 setup op per prime per sublattice (TREE_SIEVE_HONEST) and windowing only the final prime of random branch choice into (2K^2/d_{m-1}, 4K^2/d_{m-1}] (RND_WINDOW) at identical 72,000 pooled work (72-96 bits) gave: RND_WINDOW 10 hits (9 main-shell, 2,216 tested, 57.8 ms), beating BEAM_QNORM (5/4, 102.5 ms), BEAM_SV (4/4, 131.6 ms), PROXY_TILT (3/3), MCMC_PROXY (2/2), TREE_SIEVE_HONEST (1/1, 26.5 ms), and MITM_COLLIDE (1/0). At penultimate depth m-1, r = -0.0503 (n = 1,674), and below the proved Non-Visibility bound d_{m-1} < 3(K/4)^2/4, 0 of 725 penultimate shortest Q-vectors equaled the terminal point (r = -0.0981).

7. NEXT STEP

Hatch 1's M2 branch-selection route is closed across certificate portfolios, MITM, partial-norm pruning, tree-sieving, and local branch walks; the remaining unrestricted-circuit gap is P-vs-NP-equivalent. Redirect effort to Open Lemma 2 (growing-rank threshold lemma for LP_exc^rel) and Open Lemma 3 (rank-to-fresh-residual lemma for shared descent in DAG-POOL, notes/hatch_results.md:131-141).
